001 | Full — bundled with HLR-1 (same priority, same surface): the focus-indicator consistency work edits the same controls and modals as the operability fixes; a split would double-touch every audited component. |\n111\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Full — VPAT and accessibility statement update, republication, and technical review. P2 per docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Normal → P2). |\n112\t\n113\tAll three HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n114\t\n115\t## Health Warnings\n116\t\n117\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n118\t  Dependencies table, no formal Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no NFRs.\n119\t  Epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and problem statement instead.\n120\t- **Unspecified Documentation owner**: the strategy names no team for the\n121\t  Documentation component. E002 is assigned to \"RHOAI Documentation\" as the\n122\t  owning team; confirm before scheduling.\n123\t- **Audit report as external input**: the per-flow defect enumeration is\n124\t  referenced but not embedded in the strategy. E001's scope and ACs are defined\n125\t  relative to that report; if the report is unavailable at execution time, the\n126\t  implementing team must re-derive the defect list (captured as an AC input, not\n127\t  an Investigation — the answer changes no epic structure).\n128\t- No priority inversions and no scope traps detected. Both epics are\n129\t  well within a sprint of effort; no re-split triggers needed.\n130\t\n131\t## Tiered Delivery\n132\t\n133\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. The customer\n134\trequirement couples the fixes and the published VPAT, but the docs epic is a\n135\tnormal Rule 11 terminal node, not a tiered Tier 1/Tier 2 split.\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84dbaa99ca0a4a8d","agent_id":"af28a357f83e49d35","session_id":"84025926-e088-44a2-8c99-e9522ae1f2f6","uuid":"f909ffb0-782f-4416-8a69-a444b1fe4ccb","time